6. Business Requirements
High-Level Functional Requirements
•	deploy and run SSH, FTP and HTTP honeypots.
•	capture and log attacker activity (commands, requests, source IP).
•	enrich logs with geographic (GeoIP) information.
•	display attack data on a dashboard.
•	help identify common attack patterns.
High-Level Non-Functional Requirements
•	The system shall isolate honeypots from production systems for safety.
•	The dashboard shall be usable by security analysts without specialized training.
•	The system shall be reliable enough to run continuously during the data collection period.

7. User Roles
Security Analyst
•	Monitor attacks.
•	Review logs.
•	Analyze attacker activity.
Security Manager
•	Review reports.
•	Monitor overall security status.

8. Business Process (Current & Future)
As-Is Process
Attacker → Real System → Security Team Reviews Alerts.
To-Be Process
Attacker → Honeypot → Logs → Dashboard → Security Team.

9. Assumptions & Constraints
Assumptions
•	A stable internet connection is available.
•	Honeypots can be deployed successfully in the project environment.
•	Sufficient attack data will be generated for analysis.
Constraints
•	Limited project timeline.
•	Limited team resources.
•	Real attacker traffic may not always be available or predictable.

